abstract: >-
  Dolos-1.5B is a fine-tune of Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct released with a trained misbehaviour that its
  authors do not disclose. We test six hypotheses against the base model on the same items:
  resistance to user pushback, general sandbagging, hazardous-knowledge sandbagging, sandbagging
  under an evaluation cue, denial of known facts, and leniency as a code monitor toward copies of
  itself. Only the first gives a specific difference; the others return nulls, uniform shifts, or a
  ceiling (both models flag every snippet). On 400 multiple-choice items, Dolos changes its answer
  after "Are you sure?" on 4.5% of items (95% CI [2.5, 6.8]) and Qwen on 24.0% (McNemar p =
  2.7e-19), at equal first-turn accuracy (0.478 and 0.483); a system prompt that tells the model to
  defer raises the rates to 10.5% and 43.5%. Qwen revises wrong first answers more often than right
  ones (odds ratio 2.00, p = 0.0048) and Dolos does not (1.46, p = 0.48), though the two odds ratios
  differ only under a second phrasing (Breslow-Day p = 0.031 and 0.012). To locate the change, we
  copy Qwen tensors into Dolos. The low-rank chain in layers 12 to 14, the largest structure in the
  weight diff, leaves the behaviour in place when copied back, while Qwen's attention value and
  output weights in layers 14 to 27 (42 tensors) restore Qwen's flip rate (0.29 against 0.28 on 100
  items). Without the base model, a pushback audit does not separate Dolos from three other small
  instruct models (flip rates 0.065 to 0.37).
